{
  "dom_id": "question-126770",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Calculate the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,⋯\\,.$",
  "worked_solution": "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nWriting the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{120}{x}^{10}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x-{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{9}+⋯\\,.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
